WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.884
[MUSIC] My name is Anat Admati,

00:00:01.884 --> 00:00:03.880
I'm a professor of finance and

00:00:03.880 --> 00:00:05.570
economics at the Stanford

00:00:05.570 --> 00:00:07.797
Graduate School of Business, and

00:00:07.797 --> 00:00:10.272
I teach interdisciplinary courses.

00:00:10.272 --> 00:00:12.633
&gt;&gt; And I'm Rob Siegel, a lecturer

00:00:12.633 --> 00:00:15.257
and management here at the Stanford

00:00:15.257 --> 00:00:17.535
Graduate School of Business.

00:00:17.535 --> 00:00:19.463
I teach a variety of classes here

00:00:19.463 --> 00:00:20.396
at the school.

00:00:20.396 --> 00:00:21.724
And today Anat and I are going to

00:00:21.724 --> 00:00:23.439
talk about our course, business and

00:00:23.439 --> 00:00:25.044
governments power engagements in

00:00:25.044 --> 00:00:26.324
the 21st century world.

00:00:26.324 --> 00:00:33.144
[MUSIC]

00:00:33.144 --> 00:00:35.020
&gt;&gt; Our first point is that markets

00:00:35.020 --> 00:00:37.113
can work well without functioning

00:00:37.113 --> 00:00:39.142
governments and legal systems.

00:00:39.142 --> 00:00:40.966
We take a lot for granted around

00:00:40.966 --> 00:00:42.305
here at Stanford GSB.

00:00:42.305 --> 00:00:44.332
We think operations just exist, we

00:00:44.332 --> 00:00:46.291
think that there are transactions

00:00:46.291 --> 00:00:47.978
and there are contracts, and

00:00:47.978 --> 00:00:49.937
there is competition in markets,

00:00:49.937 --> 00:00:51.027
all these things.

00:00:51.027 --> 00:00:53.234
And we don't start to think what

00:00:53.234 --> 00:00:54.633
makes that happen.

00:00:54.633 --> 00:00:55.801
How is it all these things

00:00:55.801 --> 00:00:56.415
come about?

00:00:56.415 --> 00:00:57.933
And what do you do if somebody

00:00:57.933 --> 00:00:59.705
breaks a promise that they made to

00:00:59.705 --> 00:01:01.541
you doesn't pay you a right time,

00:01:01.541 --> 00:01:02.934
or prevents you from doing

00:01:02.934 --> 00:01:04.519
something after you finished

00:01:04.519 --> 00:01:06.355
working for them, because of some

00:01:06.355 --> 00:01:07.836
non-compete agreement?

00:01:07.836 --> 00:01:08.718
Where do you go?

00:01:08.718 --> 00:01:10.070
You need a system to ensure

00:01:10.070 --> 00:01:11.920
that all these things are working.

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:17.319
[MUSIC]

00:01:17.319 --> 00:01:19.352
Leaders in business and leaders in

00:01:19.352 --> 00:01:21.524
governments must remember that what

00:01:21.524 --> 00:01:23.205
they do can protect lives and

00:01:23.205 --> 00:01:24.256
livelihoods, or

00:01:24.256 --> 00:01:26.091
it can also harm and endanger.

00:01:26.091 --> 00:01:27.833
We have numerous examples,

00:01:27.833 --> 00:01:29.430
where we love products and

00:01:29.430 --> 00:01:30.810
markets work great and

00:01:30.810 --> 00:01:32.844
everybody's doing their bit and

00:01:32.844 --> 00:01:33.945
it all works out.

00:01:33.945 --> 00:01:35.787
But in this class, we also saw

00:01:35.787 --> 00:01:37.925
examples of both business leaders

00:01:37.925 --> 00:01:39.555
and government leaders.

00:01:39.555 --> 00:01:41.179
Hey people, for example,

00:01:41.179 --> 00:01:43.225
we saw a corporation sold a product

00:01:43.225 --> 00:01:45.132
that became clear was unsafe for

00:01:45.132 --> 00:01:47.392
children and yet, they don't inform

00:01:47.392 --> 00:01:48.734
consumers about it and

00:01:48.734 --> 00:01:50.216
they leave the product on

00:01:50.216 --> 00:01:51.646
the market for a while.

00:01:51.646 --> 00:01:53.798
And even when they're we call it,

00:01:53.798 --> 00:01:56.026
they don't take very much action to

00:01:56.026 --> 00:01:57.744
remove it from the market.

00:01:57.744 --> 00:01:59.880
And the government doesn't disclose

00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:01.815
that information very broadly and

00:02:01.815 --> 00:02:03.750
doesn't force the corporation to

00:02:03.750 --> 00:02:05.361
recall more aggressively.

00:02:05.361 --> 00:02:10.195
[MUSIC]

00:02:10.195 --> 00:02:11.648
&gt;&gt; Third, we found that leaders

00:02:11.648 --> 00:02:12.402
need to find and

00:02:12.402 --> 00:02:14.146
challenge assumptions and wishful

00:02:14.146 --> 00:02:15.726
thinking including their own.

00:02:15.726 --> 00:02:16.532
For example,

00:02:16.532 --> 00:02:18.279
we saw that assumptions people

00:02:18.279 --> 00:02:20.227
would make in how they would assume

00:02:20.227 --> 00:02:22.176
markets work and where market will

00:02:22.176 --> 00:02:23.855
fail were not always clear and

00:02:23.855 --> 00:02:25.199
obvious when people dug

00:02:25.199 --> 00:02:27.166
down beneath their assumptions.

00:02:27.166 --> 00:02:28.895
Similarly, we also found that

00:02:28.895 --> 00:02:30.091
the capabilities and

00:02:30.091 --> 00:02:32.020
competencies that were assumed of

00:02:32.020 --> 00:02:34.015
leaders in business and government

00:02:34.015 --> 00:02:35.877
were not always matched up to what

00:02:35.877 --> 00:02:37.871
people thought was the world versus

00:02:37.871 --> 00:02:39.748
what the reality actually held.

00:02:39.748 --> 00:02:42.787
[MUSIC]

00:02:42.787 --> 00:02:44.605
Fourth, we found that leaders need

00:02:44.605 --> 00:02:46.121
to beware of false choices and

00:02:46.121 --> 00:02:47.880
misleading labels that prevent us

00:02:47.880 --> 00:02:49.456
from getting at core issues and

00:02:49.456 --> 00:02:51.034
understanding what's really

00:02:51.034 --> 00:02:52.300
being discussed.

00:02:52.300 --> 00:02:54.912
For example, words like capitalism

00:02:54.912 --> 00:02:57.910
and socialism, regulation and laws.

00:02:57.910 --> 00:02:58.802
What are costly and

00:02:58.802 --> 00:03:00.196
what are helpful and needed,

00:03:00.196 --> 00:03:01.758
even trying to understand things

00:03:01.758 --> 00:03:03.330
like critical infrastructure.

00:03:03.330 --> 00:03:04.806
Is it best served by government or

00:03:04.806 --> 00:03:05.649
is it best served by

00:03:05.649 --> 00:03:06.606
the private sector?

00:03:06.606 --> 00:03:08.455
And how does it serve the citizens

00:03:08.455 --> 00:03:10.305
of a particular country or area or

00:03:10.305 --> 00:03:11.140
nation?

00:03:11.140 --> 00:03:12.351
This can get in the way of our

00:03:12.351 --> 00:03:13.150
understanding.

00:03:13.150 --> 00:03:14.394
What are the core issues that we're

00:03:14.394 --> 00:03:15.105
trying to address?

00:03:15.105 --> 00:03:16.597
And really, understand what are we

00:03:16.597 --> 00:03:18.040
trying to accomplish as humans and

00:03:18.040 --> 00:03:18.644
as a society.

00:03:18.644 --> 00:03:22.379
[MUSIC]

00:03:22.379 --> 00:03:24.338
&gt;&gt; Our fifth point is that

00:03:24.338 --> 00:03:27.228
effective governance harnesses

00:03:27.228 --> 00:03:30.632
power, information, and incentives

00:03:30.632 --> 00:03:33.950
to create trustworthy systems.

00:03:33.950 --> 00:03:35.137
We talked in this class about

00:03:35.137 --> 00:03:36.523
institutions, institutions of

00:03:36.523 --> 00:03:37.958
government, institutions in the

00:03:37.958 --> 00:03:39.810
private sectors like corporations.

00:03:39.810 --> 00:03:41.322
And in these institutions,

00:03:41.322 --> 00:03:43.212
there are leaders that have power,

00:03:43.212 --> 00:03:44.724
that have information, and

00:03:44.724 --> 00:03:45.984
they have discretion or

00:03:45.984 --> 00:03:46.910
a way to act.

00:03:46.910 --> 00:03:49.238
Good governance means that they

00:03:49.238 --> 00:03:51.392
are trustworthy and that they

00:03:51.392 --> 00:03:53.897
are accountable for what they do.

00:03:53.897 --> 00:03:55.747
That is the opposite of abuse of

00:03:55.747 --> 00:03:56.316
power and

00:03:56.316 --> 00:03:58.105
corruption, you might say.

00:03:58.105 --> 00:04:00.320
So creating trustworthy system

00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:02.371
means creating institutions

00:04:02.371 --> 00:04:04.344
that have good governance.

00:04:04.344 --> 00:04:05.619
And if we do that,

00:04:05.619 --> 00:04:07.830
then we can thrive as a societ.

00:04:07.830 --> 00:04:11.161
[MUSIC]

00:04:11.161 --> 00:04:12.782
&gt;&gt; Or not and

00:04:12.782 --> 00:04:16.632
I bring very different

00:04:16.632 --> 00:04:21.700
backgrounds experiences and

00:04:21.700 --> 00:04:28.120
trained to these important topics.

00:04:28.120 --> 00:04:29.708
One thing though, is very true,

00:04:29.708 --> 00:04:30.786
I'm not an idealist,

00:04:30.786 --> 00:04:32.540
disagree on everything.

00:04:32.540 --> 00:04:33.801
&gt;&gt; No Rob, you're wrong,

00:04:33.801 --> 00:04:35.610
we don't disagree on everything.

00:04:35.610 --> 00:04:37.760
We're agree many things actually.

00:04:37.760 --> 00:04:39.695
&gt;&gt; Okay, eventually yes, we do end

00:04:39.695 --> 00:04:41.363
up agreeing on everything.

00:04:41.363 --> 00:04:44.283
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:04:44.283 --> 00:04:54.283
[MUSIC]

